  <!-- Skip Link -->
  <main id="main-content" class="seo-page"><section class="seo-hero"><p class="seo-kicker">Remote care enquiry</p><h1>Ask whether teleconsultation is suitable</h1>
<div class="seo-answer"><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Teleconsultation at Mind Grace follows the Telemedicine Practice Guidelines of India (2020). Some follow-up and counselling conversations may be possible remotely, but remote care is not suitable for every person, concern, or situation &mdash; suitability is decided case by case.</div><div class="seo-actions"><a class="btn" href="/contact">Ask the clinic</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="/book">Request an appointment</a></div></section><section class="seo-section"><h2>Before a remote appointment</h2><div class="seo-grid"><article class="seo-card"><h3>Eligibility</h3><p>The clinic must confirm clinician availability, location, identity, consent, privacy, and whether remote care is clinically appropriate.</p></article><article class="seo-card"><h3>Privacy</h3><p>Use a private room and a reliable connection. Share only the information requested through the clinic’s approved booking channel.</p></article><article class="seo-card"><h3>Limits</h3><p>Remote care may require an in-person review, examination, testing, referral, or local emergency support.</p></article></div><div class="seo-note"><strong>Emergency warning:</strong> Do not use teleconsultation, email, WhatsApp, or this website for immediate danger or crisis support. Call 112 or go to the nearest emergency department.</div></section><section class="seo-section"><h2>Consent and follow-up</h2><p>Ask how consent, prescriptions, records, fees, cancellations, and follow-up will work before the consultation. Read the clinic’s <a href="/privacy">privacy information</a>.</p></section>
  <section class="seo-section"><h2>How to request an online consultation</h2><p>Start by contacting Mind Grace through the <a href="/book">appointment request page</a> or the published phone number. Tell the team that you are asking about an online consultation and share only the basic information needed to coordinate the enquiry. The clinic will confirm whether a clinician is available and whether remote care is suitable for your concern. Submitting a form or opening a WhatsApp chat does not confirm a video appointment.</p><p>Some appointments may be more appropriate in person, particularly when an examination, assessment tool, observation or local support is needed. A remote visit may also need to change to an in-person review if the clinician cannot safely answer the question online. The decision depends on the clinician’s judgement, the person’s circumstances and current service availability.</p><h2>Prepare a private and reliable setting</h2><ul><li>Choose a private room where you can speak without being overheard.</li><li>Check your connection, device battery, camera and audio before the agreed time.</li><li>Keep a phone number available in case the call disconnects.</li><li>Have a list of current medicines and relevant reports nearby if the clinician has asked for them.</li><li>If you need another person present, discuss this with the clinic and the clinician, including consent and privacy.</li></ul><p>Use a secure and private connection where possible. Avoid joining from a public place or sharing links to a consultation. Ask the clinic which platform it uses and how appointment details are sent. Do not include detailed medical history in ordinary email or booking messages; use the channel the clinic specifies for clinical information.</p><h2>Consent, records and prescriptions</h2><p>Before the consultation, ask how consent is recorded, what happens if the connection fails, how records are maintained, and how follow-up is arranged. Fees and cancellation terms should be confirmed before the appointment; see the <a href="/fees">fee page</a> and ask whether the rate differs by appointment type. A prescription or referral is not guaranteed simply because the appointment takes place online. The clinician determines what can be managed remotely and whether an in-person assessment or other service is needed.</p><p>Read the clinic’s <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a> for information about personal details. If you are calling about a child, use the <a href="/aasha">Aasha pathway</a> and ask its team whether a remote conversation is appropriate for the child’s situation.</p><h2>When an online consultation is not the right route</h2><p>Teleconsultation is not an emergency or crisis service. Do not wait for a scheduled video call if someone may be in immediate danger, has a serious injury, or needs urgent medical attention. Call 112 or go to the nearest emergency department. If a concern requires local examination or immediate support, the clinician may recommend an in-person visit or referral.</p><p>For routine planning, review <a href="/psychiatry">psychiatry consultations</a>, <a href="/therapy">therapy options</a>, <a href="/fees">current fees</a> and <a href="/process">first-visit information</a>. The clinic will confirm the practical details and suitability of any remote appointment before a time is agreed.</p></section></main>
</Layout>
